  "missingStatements": [
    {
      "text": "Polluters should be legally liable to compensate or stop for any proven physical harm to other people's property, rather than being regulated by agencies.",
      "value": 2,
      "rationale": "Rothbard and Anderson-Leal treat pollution as a tort to be settled by private suits and injunctions, replacing regulation.",
      "sources": [
        "s2",
        "s4"
      ],
      "addedAs": "pollution-tort-liability"
    },
    {
      "text": "Natural resources such as air, water and ecosystems should be divided into privately owned and tradeable property rights.",
      "value": 2,
      "rationale": "Free-market environmentalism proposes assigning enforceable property and contract rights over environmental resources, and Walshe lets people trade their share of natural services.",
      "sources": [
        "s4",
        "s1"
      ],
      "addedAs": "nature-as-capital"
    },
    {
      "text": "Nobody may use natural resources beyond a fair share without the consent of others affected.",
      "value": 2,
      "rationale": "Walshe's ecological Lockean proviso requires consent for use of natural services beyond one's share.",
      "sources": [
        "s1"
      ],
      "declined": "Walshe's ecological proviso is specific to one thinker and is covered in part by future-generations."
    }
  ],
  "notes": "Wikipedia notes that green libertarians are a minority among green political theorists and the article flags notability concerns. Walshe's original paper was not opened, only the Wikipedia summary. Geolibertarian and left-libertarian green positions (land-value tax, basic income) would answer several economic statements differently. Many answers are inferences from libertarianism generally.",
